      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
          "content": "Cancer pain in the terminally ill often poses great therapeutic dilemma. Opioids, whilst being useful in most cases, often leaves a patient heavily sedated and constipated at high doses and sometimes, in persistent agony from cancer pain."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL PICTURE",
          "content": "An Indian lady who suffered from metastatic carcinoma of the cervix experienced tremendous pain and disability despite high doses of narcotics and membrane stabilizers."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "TREATMENT",
          "content": "A ganglion of impar block and superior hypogastric plexus block were performed with a neurolytic agent."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "OUTCOME",
          "content": "The patient's pain and opioids usage were markedly reduced."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Neurolytic nerve block can offer a great therapeutic option in selected cancer patients."
        }
      ]},
